the difference between games and other  forms of media is a pretty interesting  one and while games can obviously be  unique and different in all sorts of  ways it's fascinating to see what can  happen when they take inspiration from  another medium so that said let's talk  about games that take inspiration from  cinema we have games that feel like TV  shows such as tell tales episodic  masterpieces and games were just  straight-up adaptations of films or  books this group of games though is  something different some of them are  working to incorporate the cinematic  look into their games by using an  intentional art direction to get a game  to look like it was shot on film for the  big screen take the order 1886 for  example developer ready at dawn said  that achieving this kind of constant  cinematic escapism is what they thought  to find a next-gen game rather than  high-definition graphics at 60 frames  per second the cinematic look of games  may be easier to achieve given the  current state of Technology but we've  also seen a bunch of games take on a  cinematic style in the way that they  tell stories take Metal Gear Solid for  example this series has always been  infamous for its many lengthy cutscenes  Metal Gear has one continuous epic story  to tell and much like watching all the  harry potter movies in the space of a  week playing through the series feels  like you're along for one big convoluted  ride although Metal Gear creator Hideo  Kojima expressed his interest to make  the cut things more interactive as the  series progressed and wants to minimize  cutscenes in the next installment the  phantom pain there's no denying that the  middle gear series wears its cinematic  inspiration on its sleeve James Bond  films and escape from New York are the  most obvious in an endless list Metal  Gear Solid takes their epic secret  espionage action mission tropes as  gospel and recreates them with polygons  and spreads the characters a big boss  and solid snake were basically directly  lifted from Sean Connery and Kurt  Russell and what better way to perfectly  capture all those juicy double crossings  conspiracy theory  and dastardly plans to take over the  world as well as those classic films  lots of cutscenes of course Metal Gear  Solid actually holds a Guinness world  record for the longest cutscene sequence  in gaming sitting at 71 minutes although  when you're capping off a story that  spans over 10 games that's not too bad  if you were to take a metal gear solid  game and get rid of all the awesome  gameplay sections the pacing delivery  and narrative structure would still let  you have a pretty good time in fact  Konami did just that a cutscene only  version of Metal Gear Solid 3 was  included as a bonus feature for the game  subsistence free release you see a  similar sort of cinematic implementation  with games like Uncharted and heavy rain  I'm childhood is let's face it basically  Indiana Jones with a slightly hotter  protagonist as for heavy rain although  it features a branching narrative it's  carefully crafted cinematography and  method of storytelling which paste  itself similar to film while still being  flexible is prevalent throughout the  game and finally cinema has invaded  gaming not simply in how it looks or how  the story is being told but also who is  being cast in the title roles and how  performance is captured David cage is  taken a shine to casting big-name actors  in original roles within his games most  recently casting Ellen Page and Willem  Dafoe in beyond 2 souls Quantic Dream's  latest quick timing creation the  massively cinematic le noir prominently  starred aaron staton from madman and  freaking Kevin Spacey was in Call of  Duty advanced warfare for God's sake  these games and many others like them  move beyond voice work and also utilize  motion and facial capture technologies  to try and accurately represent the  likeness of the actors in order to fully  embody the characters you may not have  recognized all the voice actors who  worked on the last of us well apart from  Troy Baker and not Ellen Page but the  well written dialogue and spot-on  performances were deemed to be so  engaging they performed it live on stage  let me know if you reckon games can  learn anything from movies or if you  even like games that go for that  cinematic feel leave me your comments  and I will catch you next week
 

